{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmZ5cEhLDfAfDkuDAqLL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I approved all three rows (`OUTCOME: APPROVE rows=3`) and committed the approval. The verdict is committed as `baf728ee` with the `WI: WI-835` trailer, in `docs/reviews/wi-835-coordinator-retained-adjudication/005-ADJUDICATE-b0aab2c.md`.\n\nThe three findings I returned last round are all fixed:\n- **SR-231:** its acceptance no longer asks for creating missing parent directories or releasing the reservation on a refusal. What's left are observable checks of its two requirements: launch only from a claimed lane, and succeed only on a verdict this call wrote within its deadline. \"Only one concurrent call can claim a verdict path\" is what makes a verdict this call's own, so it belongs there.\n- **LLR-306:** it now traces to SR-227 as well as SR-231, and its rationale says which part serves which. It uses LLR-305's shared request rather than restating it, and points to IF-283 and IF-284 by id. Its sign-in command is tested by an approved case, TC-323.\n- **TC-321:** it now lists SR-227 among what it verifies, which matches what it asserts. The two coordinator-route lifecycle tests are back in its evidence, and its Expected states the \"second coordinator-route adjudication resumes\" condition and the drain at the next clear point. All 17 tests it cites exist.\n\nThe approval commit is `13a8453f`. It changes only those three rows from Drafted to Approved, and snapshots the SR, LLR and TC registries, recorded as act 40.\n\nEvery row in WI-835's SR-227 and SR-231 chains is now approved and anchored."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":518,"cache_read_input_tokens":183717,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":518},"output_tokens":550,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"